# TIL — 2026-05-03: structuredClone for safe deep copies

structuredClone is a native, fast deep-clone available in modern browsers and Node. It preserves Dates, RegExps, Maps, Sets, ArrayBuffers, etc., but not functions or custom prototype methods. Supports Transferables for zero-copy transfers.

```js
// simple deep clone
const original = {d: new Date(), m: new Map([['x',1]])};
const copy = structuredClone(original);
console.log(copy.d instanceof Date, copy.m instanceof Map); // true, true

// functions/prototype are not preserved
const proto = {greet(){return 'hi'}};
const obj = Object.create(proto);
obj.fn = ()=>42;
const cloned = structuredClone(obj);
console.log(cloned.greet); // undefined
console.log(typeof cloned.fn); // 'undefined'

// Transferable example (zero-copy for ArrayBuffer)
const buf = new ArrayBuffer(1024);
const bufClone = structuredClone(buf, {transfer: [buf]});
console.log(buf.byteLength, bufClone.byteLength); // 0, 1024
```

Fallback: use JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x)) when structuredClone isn't available (loses Dates/Maps/undefined/functions).